# Supplemental Material texts for R18 (moved from the main text)

S1 = ("Section S1", "The Original Strong/Weak Classification of the V1s", [
"When the materials were designed, the V1s were classified as Strong and Weak V1s, following Martin et al. (2020, 2026). On this classification, Strong V1s, such as *xǐ* \"wash,\" strongly imply a change of state in the object, whereas Weak V1s, such as *mō* \"pet,\" at best weakly imply one. The two classes are distinguished by a single diagnostic: Only Strong V1s are compatible with a preverbal time-span adverbial, as in *yì fēnzhōng xǐ-le yì shuāng wàzi* \"washed a pair of socks in one minute\" versus \\**yì fēnzhōng mō-le yì zhī māo* \"petted a cat in one minute.\" Some V1s in the present materials were taken from Martin et al. (2020), and the others were selected with this diagnostic.",
"We no longer use this classification, for three reasons. First, the diagnostic is binary, whereas the property it was meant to capture, the strength with which a V1 implies a result, is a matter of degree. Second, the diagnostic tracks whether a result construal of the verb phrase is available, which the object and the context can supply, rather than a fixed property of the V1: Several of the Weak V1s in the present materials are also compatible with the adverbial when combined with a suitable object, as in *yì fēnzhōng tuī-le yí ge xiāngzi* \"pushed a box in one minute,\" and the same holds for *chōng* \"rinse\" and *chuí* \"hammer.\" Third, the Strong V1s grouped verbs that name an action directed at a specific result, such as *bāo* \"peel,\" *sī* \"tear,\" and *cā* \"wipe,\" with verbs that name a manner of action compatible with many results, such as *tī* \"kick\" and *chuī* \"blow,\" and the diagnostic provides no independent reason to group them together. Because the diagnostic characterizes verbs rather than the relation between an action and a particular result, the present study characterizes the pairs by adult ratings of each action's association with its target result.",
])

S2 = ("Section S2", "Verb–Video Correspondence Norming", [
"Table S1 gives, for each video, the proportion of norming participants whose first description contained the intended V1 and the proportion of all descriptions that contained it. Across videos, the intended V1 appeared in 74% of first descriptions and in 72% of all descriptions. When it did not appear after two attempts, participants rated how natural the intended V1 was as a description of the action (1 = *very unnatural*, 5 = *very natural*; *M* = 4.32, *SD* = 0.79, range = 2.67–5).",
])

S3 = ("Section S3", "Adult Ratings: Procedure and Analysis", [
"The questionnaire took about 5 min. After a consent statement and questions about their language background (native language, frequency of Mandarin use, and English proficiency), raters were told that the questions had no correct answers and were asked to respond on the basis of their everyday experience and first intuition. In the likelihood block, each item presented a perfective sentence containing the V1–object expression, followed by a question about the target result, for example, *Xiǎo Lǐ sī-le zhǐ* \"Xiao Li tore the paper. How likely do you think it is that the paper ended up torn apart?\" The instructions asked raters to judge, on the basis of the sentence and their everyday experience, how likely it was that the stated result eventually occurred. They typed a number from 0 to 100, where 0 was labeled *cannot happen* and 100 *will certainly happen*. In the typicality block, the instructions stated that each item first named a result that someone wanted to achieve and then an action that could be used to achieve it, and asked raters to rate how typical the action was as a way of achieving that result, for example, \"Suppose Xiao Li wants to crack the walnut open. How typical is *jiā hétao* 'clamping the walnut' as a way of doing this?\" Responses were given on a 7-point scale whose endpoints were labeled 1 (*very atypical*) and 7 (*very typical*); the intermediate points were numbered but not labeled. The likelihood items thus presented each expression in the same perfective form as the V1 questions of Experiment 1.",
"Four lists were constructed, and five raters completed each list. In all lists, the 20 likelihood items preceded the 20 typicality items. Within each block, the 10 stronger-association expressions and the 10 weaker-association expressions were presented in separate halves, with the pairs in the same order in both halves, so that the two members of each pair were always separated by nine other items. The stronger-association expressions came first in Lists 1 and 3, and the weaker-association expressions came first in Lists 2 and 4. Four different pair orders were used, two for Lists 1 and 2 and two for Lists 3 and 4, and the two blocks of each list used different orders. Because the likelihood block always came first, the typicality judgments may have been influenced by the preceding likelihood judgments. All raters completed all items, and no ratings were excluded.",
"For each rater and pair, we computed the difference between the ratings of the two expressions (expected stronger- minus expected weaker-association expression) and modeled these differences with crossed random intercepts for raters and pairs, estimated by restricted maximum likelihood. Because there were only 10 pairs, 95% confidence intervals were obtained from a bootstrap that resampled both raters and pairs, and exact *p* values from a randomization test that exchanged the two expression labels within pairs across all 2^10^ assignments. The correlation between the pair-level differences in the two ratings was tested with a permutation test. For pair selection, each pair was assessed with a within-rater label-exchange test (*p* < .05, uncorrected).",
"Two pairs did not meet the selection criterion. In Pair 7 (*qiā* \"pinch\" vs. *jiào* \"call\" a person, with the target result *xǐng* \"awake\"), the likelihood difference was in the expected direction, but the typicality difference was reversed (−1.00 points, 95% CI [−1.70, −0.30]). In Pair 8 (*tú* \"paint\" vs. *náo* \"scratch\" a palm, with the target result *hóng* \"red\"), neither difference was reliable (likelihood: 16.40 points, 95% CI [−1.00, 33.40]; typicality: 0.45 points, 95% CI [−0.90, 1.85]).",
])

S4 = ("Section S4", "Corpus Search Procedure", [
"The modern Chinese section of the CCL corpus (Zhan et al., 2019) reports the number of documents in which a string occurs rather than the number of tokens, and string matching also returns sequences in which the two characters belong to different words (e.g., *zhǐhuī* | *mièhuǒ* \"command | fire-fighting\" for *huī-miè*). All matches for the less frequent RVCs were therefore checked by hand. For the more frequent RVCs, matches were segmented automatically to check for word boundaries before V1 and after V2, and 10–12 matches per RVC were checked by hand; for *tú-hóng* \"paint-red,\" a sample of 60 matches was checked. Figurative and idiomatic uses (e.g., *chě-kāi sǎngzi* \"pull-open throat,\" \"raise one's voice\") were counted, because they also indicate that the combination is conventional. For the RVCs checked by sampling, the number of documents was estimated by multiplying the raw document count by the proportion of checked matches that were resultative uses and rounded to the nearest 10; for the other RVCs, all matches were checked.",
])

S5 = ("Section S5", "Test Sentences and Filler Questions in Experiment 1", [
"Table S2 lists the V1 and RVC questions for each action. Each question was presented after the full-result or the zero-result video of that action. The filler questions were *Tā shǔshù le ma?* \"Did he count?\", *Tā zuò-xià le ma?* \"Did he sit down?\", *Tā yáotóu le ma?* \"Did he shake his head?\", *Tā zǒu-guòlái le ma?* \"Did he walk over?\", *Tā tiào le ma?* \"Did he jump?\", *Tā fēi-qǐlái le ma?* \"Did he fly up?\", *Tā fēi-guòlái le ma?* \"Did he fly over?\", *Tā shuōhuà le ma?* \"Did he speak?\", *Tā dǎ hāqian le ma?* \"Did he yawn?\", *Tā tǎng-xià le ma?* \"Did he lie down?\", *Tā pǎo-guòlái le ma?* \"Did he run over?\", and *Tā diǎntóu le ma?* \"Did he nod?\"; depending on the list, a filler question matched or did not match the action in the video.",
])

S6_INTRO = "The instructions are given in the original Chinese with English translations. Children and adults received the same instructions in Experiment 1."
S6 = ("Section S6", "Instructions", [
("H3", "Experiment 1"),
("P", "您好！现在我先和您简单介绍一下我们的实验。我们的实验任务非常简单，您会看一些短视频，视频中播放的是一些日常活动。在看完每个视频后，我会和您说我看到的视频的内容，您需要判断我说的对或者不对，然后写下您的答案。下面我给您举个例子。首先请您认真观看这段视频。我看到在这个视频里，这个女孩趴下了。您觉得我说得对还是不对？这个女孩趴下了吗？……好的，您还有什么其他问题吗？那我开始播放其他视频了。"),
("P", "\"Hello! Let me briefly introduce our experiment. The task is very simple: You will watch some short videos of everyday activities. After each video, I will tell you what I saw in the video, and you need to judge whether what I said is right or wrong and then give your answer. Here is an example. First, please watch this video carefully. I saw in this video that this girl lay down. Do you think what I said is right or wrong? Did this girl lie down? . . . Do you have any other questions? Then I will start playing the other videos.\" Participants typically answered by repeating or negating the verb (e.g., *pā-xià le* \"lay down\"; *méi pā-xià* \"did not lie down\"), and their answers were coded as acceptance or rejection."),
("H3", "Experiment 2: Adults"),
("P", "大家好！欢迎大家参与我们的实验，现在我先简单介绍一下我们实验的内容：我们的实验任务非常简单，大家会看一些短视频，视频中播放日常中的活动。在看完每个视频后，大家需要在答题纸上用一句话描述您在视频里看到了什么，您将有充分的时间进行描述。下面我给您举个例子。首先请认真观看这段视频。好的，如果我作为参与者，我会说我看到了：一个女孩趴下了。那我会把我的描述写到第一题的方框里，大家也可以把你们的描述写在方框里了。大家还有什么问题吗？那么下面我开始播放其他视频了。"),
("P", "\"Hello, everyone! Welcome to our experiment. Let me briefly introduce what it involves. The task is very simple: You will watch some short videos of everyday activities. After each video, please describe in one sentence on the answer sheet what you saw in the video; you will have plenty of time. Here is an example. First, please watch this video carefully. If I were a participant, I would say that I saw: A girl lay down. I would write my description in the box for the first question, and you can now write your description in the box. Do you have any questions? Then I will start playing the other videos.\""),
("H3", "Experiment 2: Children"),
("P", "今天我们一起来看一些小视频，看完每段视频之后我们来玩我问你答的游戏。老师会问你你在这个视频里你看到了什么，你帮我回答。那我们现在开始玩游戏了。"),
("P", "\"Today we are going to watch some short videos together. After each video, we will play a game in which I ask and you answer. I will ask you what you saw in the video, and you help me answer. Let's start the game now.\" Before the test trials, children were familiarized with three of the objects (a candle, a lychee, and a walnut). After each video, the experimenter asked, *Nǐ kàn-dào le shénme ya?* \"What did you see?\" If the child responded with a verb, no further question was asked. If the child named only an object, the experimenter asked, *Nà zhège rén zài zuò shénme ya?* \"Then what is this person doing?\" If the child said that they did not know, the video was played again and the first question repeated; if the child still did not know, the experimenter asked why, and the trial was not analyzed."),
])

S7_TEXT = [
"All models were fitted with brms (Bürkner, 2017) in R (R Core Team, 2024), with four chains of 3,000 iterations each, including 1,000 warmup iterations, and adapt_delta = .95. Fixed effects, including the intercept, had Normal(0, 1.5) priors, standard deviations of random effects Exponential(1) priors, and correlation matrices LKJ(2) priors. Factors were sum-coded: Form (RVC = 1, V1 = −1), Result Realization (Full = 1, Zero = −1), Action–Result Association (Stronger = 1, Weaker = −1), and Age Group (4-year-olds, 5-year-olds, and adults coded with two contrasts, adults = −1 on both). Predicted probabilities were computed from the population-level effects, with random effects set to zero. Convergence was assessed by R̂ < 1.01 and adequate effective sample sizes; all reported models had R̂ ≤ 1.01 and at most one divergent transition, except one leave-one-pair-out refit with four.",
"In lme4 notation, Model 1 (Experiment 1, RQ1) was *accept* ~ *Form* × *Result* × *Age* + (1 + *Result* | participant) + (1 + *Form* + *Result* + *Age* | item) + (1 | pair), where an item is a V1–object expression together with its RVC. Model 2 (Experiment 1, RQ2; V1 trials only) was *accept* ~ *Association* × *Result* × *Age* + (1 + *Result* | participant) + (1 + *Result* + *Age* | item) + (1 + *Association* | pair). The two models of Experiment 2 were *y* ~ *Result* × *Age* + (1 + *Result* | participant) + (1 + *Result* + *Age* | item) + (1 | pair), where *y* is whether a response contains an RVC or whether it mentions the target result, and an item is the pair of videos of one action. Table S3 gives the population-level estimates of the four main models.",
]

S10 = ("Section S10", "Coding of Experiment 2 Responses", [
"The action is coded as mentioned when any verb describes the depicted action; whether this verb is the intended V1 is recorded separately, and general verbs such as *nòng* \"do, handle\" and *gǎo* \"do\" are coded as mentioning the action without specifying it. The target result is coded as mentioned when the response contains the V2 or an expression listed as equivalent for that pair (Table S5), whether the result is affirmed or denied. Only explicitly expressed information is coded: A *bǎ* construction with a monomorphemic verb (e.g., *bǎ làzhú chuī-le* \"BA candle blow-PERF\") is coded as mentioning the action only. A response is coded as containing an RVC when an action verb is directly followed by a result complement, including potential forms with *de* or *bu* (e.g., *qiāo bù kāi* \"knock-cannot-open\").",
"A mentioned target result is coded as denied when it is preceded within the clause by a negator (*méi*, *bù*, *wèi*), as a goal when it is preceded by an attempt verb (e.g., *xiǎng* \"want,\" *shìtú* \"try,\" *chángshì* \"attempt,\" *qǐtú* \"attempt,\" *dǎsuàn* \"intend\") without a later statement of failure, as denied when such an attempt is followed by a statement of failure (e.g., *shībài* \"fail,\" *méi chénggōng* \"did not succeed\"), and as affirmed otherwise. For Pair 7, *jiào* or *hǎn* \"call\" followed by *qǐlái* \"get up\" is coded as the action (calling someone to get up), not as the result; for Pair 8, color terms describing the paint (e.g., *hóngsè de yánliào* \"red paint\") are not coded as the result.",
"⟦以上为规则编码初稿的标准：需人工逐条核对，并由第二编码者独立编码 20% 的回答后报告一致性⟧",
])

